From 2df8c4efba1ef50e63b3b920a2791ecddb992a07 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sebastian Werner <swernerx@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 20:43:11 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(parser): borrow the parent context when building a
 container sub-parser

Every block quote, list item, footnote body, definition body and JSX child
builds a sub-parser. Each one cloned `ParserOptions`, bumped two reference
counts and then initialized a 336-byte struct that is moved twice more on
its way through `parse`.

The options are now allocated once in the arena by the root parser and
borrowed by every parser of that parse, and the two pre-pass tables share
one handle instead of one each. Building a sub-source is a pointer copy
plus one nullable reference count, and `Parser` drops from 336 to 304
bytes, so both the construction and the moves touch one cache line less.

Every sub-source still parses exactly as before:

- Line comments stay off in sub-sources. `line_comments` is a per-parser
  flag that starts false on every generated sub-source, and
  `sub_parser_with_source_map` turns it back on for exactly the lines it
  could carry across the dedent -- which is what it did through the option
  field before. `ParserOptions::front_matter` needs no flag: it is read
  only where a root parser lifts metadata off a document start, and the
  definition pass now passes `None` there for the same reason its options
  copy used to clear the flag.
- Inline notes keep their override: `inline_footnotes` is the second
  per-parser flag, so a note's body still leaves a nested `^[` literal,
  and `InlineMarkerScan` is handed that flag instead of reading the shared
  option.
- Definitions and footnote labels stay shared and document-wide. They move
  under one `Rc<Prepass>` because they are collected together and shared
  together; `None` and an empty table still mean the same thing to every
  reader, so both lookups answer as they did.
- Lazy-line and comment-line sets stay per sub-source, and nesting depth
  still increases by one per container.

The definition-list probe test compares the arena's free space across the
probe instead of asserting a completely untouched arena: construction now
places the one shared copy of the options in it, while the probe itself
still allocates nothing.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
